\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background and Motivation}
Kidney stone disease represents vital global health burden with increasing incidence rates affecting approximately 10-15\% of population worldwide \cite{kittanamongkolchai2018changing,chewcharat2021trends}. Classical diagnostic methods including ultrasound, CT scans and standardized clinical biomarker analysis  require extensive manual interpretation by radiologists and clinicians which can be time consuming and subject to interobserver variability. The development of automated systems using deep learning presents opportunity to enhance the diagnostic accuracy, reduce interpretation time and improve healthcare accessibility. Recent studies in medical imaging and computational approaches has demonstrated significant potential for improving kidney stone detection along with classification offering the possibility of efficient and accurate diagnostic tools.

\subsection{Challenges and Research Contribution}
Traditional kidney stone diagnosis presents challenges including stone variability, limited access to imaging and subjective interpretation. While computational approaches have been explored, comprehensive comparisons of deep learning architectures for kidney stone classification remain limited. This research systematically evaluates three neural network architectures DNN, MLP and AE-DNN providing insights into their performance, efficiency and clinical applicability for kidney stone detection.

\subsection{Related Work in Kidney Stone Classification}
Recent research has demonstrated the effectiveness of machine learning and deep learning approaches for kidney stone classification across multiple data modalities and imaging techniques and several studies have focused on traditional machine learning approaches using custom features. Asaye et al \cite{asaye2025detection} utilizes machine learning approach to detect kidney stones from ultrasound images using 410 annotated samples employing Gabor filtering and threshold-based segmentation with GLCM texture features. Their approach obtained 98.4\% accuracy with the KNN algorithm demonstrating the potential of traditional feature based methods.  Complementing imaging approaches, research using clinical variables has also shown promise. Orlando Iparraguirre Villanueva et al \cite{iparraguirre2025data} evaluated multiple ML algorithms using non-imaging clinical parameters with Logistic Regression achieving 78\% accuracy. At the same time Panda et al \cite{10962624} reported 93\% accuracy using urine analysis data which included physical features such as pH and calcium concentration. In the domain of ultrasound image processing Khan et al \cite{khan2022detection} developed automated detection system employing median filtering for noise reduction and adaptive thresholding for segmentation, achieving 96.82\% accuracy and 92.16\% sensitivity. Their method demonstrated computational efficiency while overcoming challenges like speckle noise and low contrast in ultrasound images. Deep learning approaches have shown particular promise in kidney stone classification. Kumar et al \cite{kumar2024hybrid} proposed hybrid CNN-ResNet model that achieved 90.9\% accuracy by utilizing CNN feature extraction capabilities and ResNet deep learning advantages. Further advancing deep learning applications Yubao Liu et al \cite{liu2025integrated} developed the integrated radiomics and deep learning model for classifying kidney stone types on CT urography achieving exceptional performance withAUC of 0.95. Jacob et al \cite{jacob2025optimised} compared three deep learning models for kidney stone identification from CT scans finding that ResNet50 outperformed both VGG16 and standard CNN by 7.7\% and 4.5\% respectively. Despite these advancements there is a need that remains for comprehensive comparative studies of different neural network architectures specifically optimized for kidney stone classification using clinical biomarkers and imaging data. Our research addresses this gap by providing extensive evaluation of DNN, MLP and AE-DNN architectures on a substantial dataset of kidney images offering insights into their relative strengths, limitations and clinical applicability for kidney stone detection. This study builds upon existing work by systematically comparing architectural approaches and providing detailed performance metrics relevant to clinical deployment.





