\subsection{Dataset Description and Preprocessing}

\subsubsection{Data Collection and Characteristics}
The study utilized the publicly available Kidney Stone Classification and Object Detection dataset comprising 9,416 high-quality annotated images categorized into two distinct classes: \textbf{Normal} (4,708 images) and \textbf{Stone} (4,708 images). The perfect 50:50 class balance reflects intentional dataset construction methodology where equal numbers of confirmed normal and stone cases were systematically collected from multiple clinical sources to prevent class imbalance bias during model training and ensure robust performance evaluation. The dataset exhibits perfect class balance due to careful curation and data collection from multiple clinical sources, ensuring unbiased model training and evaluation. All images were standardized to $64 \times 64$ pixel resolution with RGB color channels, resulting in input dimension of $12,288$ features per sample $(64 \times 64 \times 3)$. The 64×64 resolution was chosen after preliminary analysis confirmed that key stone characteristics remained discernible at this resolution while ensuring computational efficiency for architectural comparisonn. The resolution reduction was incorporated to ensure computational efficiency while maintaining diagnostic relevance, as confirmed by preliminary analysis which showed that stone visibility was preserved at this resolution.
