\subsubsection{Data Cleaning, Validation and Preprocessing}
Comprehensive data validation confirmed the absence of missing values across all 9,416 records. Image preprocessing involved pixel normalization to scale intensity values to the range [0,1] using the transformation:
\begin{equation}
I_{\text{normalized}} = \frac{I_{\text{original}}}{255}
\end{equation}
where $I_{\text{original}}$ signifies original pixel intensity values. This normalization ensures stable gradient computation during neural network training and accelerated convergence.

For model training, images were flattened into one-dimensional vectors to accommodate fully-connected neural network architectures:
\begin{equation}
X_{\text{flat}} = \text{flatten}(I_{\text{normalized}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{12288}
\end{equation}

The decision to flatten images rather than use convolutional architectures was motivated by research objective to compare fundamental neural network architectures and their intrinsic ability to learn discriminative patterns directly from raw pixel data and without the inductive biases inherent in convolutional approaches. This methodological choice provides baseline performance metrics for fully connected networks and establishes foundation for future comparative studies with convolutional architectures, while maintaining computational efficiency for the architectural comparison. Statistical validation included verification of clinical relevance through distribution analysis of image features. The preprocessing pipeline ensured data consistency while preserving diagnostically significant patterns essential for accurate kidney stone detection, with preliminary analysis confirming that key stone characteristics remained discernible at the 64×64 pixel resolution.

\subsubsection{Data Augmentation and Training Strategy}
Although the primary dataset did not utilize synthetic data augmentation due to its substantial size and balanced nature and the training strategy employed several techniques to enhance model robustness. The dataset was processed using TensorFlow's data pipeline with automatic caching and prefetching to optimize training efficiency:
\begin{equation}
\text{Throughput} = \frac{\text{Batch Size}}{\text{Processing Time}} \quad \text{with} \quad \text{Batch Size} = 32
\end{equation}

The training validation split maintained strict separation to prevent data leakage, with validation set serving as independent benchmark for model selection and hyperparameter tuning. Cross validation was implemented through use of separate validation and test sets providing robust performance estimation. The dataset inherent quality and comprehensive coverage of pathological variations reduced the necessity for extensive augmentation, allowing models to learn from authentic clinical representations. This approach preserved the real-world distribution characteristics crucial for clinical deployment reliability.

