\section{Results and Analysis}

\subsection{Overall Performance Comparison}

\subsubsection{Accuracy Analysis Across Models}
The comprehensive evaluation of three deep learning architectures revealed superior performance in kidney stone classification with all models achieving remarkably high accuracy levels, as detailed in Table~\ref{tab:kidney_comprehensive_analysis}. The Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) emerged as the top performer with validation accuracy of 99.67\% closely followed by the Autoencoder-based DNN (AE-DNN) at 99.47\% accuracy. The Deep Neural Network demonstrated strong performance with 98.95\% accuracy establishing a high baseline for comparison. The performance hierarchy demonstrates that MLP consistently outperforms other architectures across multiple metrics, particularly excelling in accuracy (99.67\%), precision (99.67\%) and F1-score (99.67\%).


\subsubsection{Computational Efficiency Analysis}
Analysis of training efficiency stated that the significant variation in computational requirements across all used architectures and the MLP architecture demonstrated optimal balance between performance and efficiency achieving the fastest training time of 0.58 seconds per epoch while maintaining 99.67\% accuracyand  DNN required moderate computational resources (6.34 seconds per epoch). At the same time AE-DNN despite its superior accuracy requried highest computational cost (8.42 seconds per epoch) due to its two stage training process which involved autoencoder pretraining. This analysis reveals the important trade off between model complexity and computational efficiency that must be considered for clinical deployment.

\subsection{Comprehensive Model Evaluation}
\subsubsection{Clinical Performance and Diagnostic Accuracy Analysis}
Clinical evaluation revealed outstanding performance across all models with MLP achieving highest sensitivity (99.67\%) and specificity (99.44\%) demonstrating superior diagnostic capability however AE-DNN showed competitive performance with 99.47\% sensitivity and 98.87\% specificity. Both models maintained low false negative rates (MLP: 0.33\%, AE-DNN: 0.53\%) ensuring reliable kidney stone detection. Confusion matrix analysis confirmed MLP optimal balance with minimal classification errors (6 false positives, 4 false negatives), making it most suitable for clinical applications. All models demonstrated strong discriminatory power with identical AUC values of 0.987. MLP maintained the highest precision (99.67\%) across all recall levels, validating deep learning's reliability for kidney stone classification. Model performance was evaluated on the validation set for model selection, with final confirmation on the independent test set ensuring rigorous evaluation standards.




