\subsection{Statistical Significance, Limitations and Clinical Relevance}
The performance across all models demonstrates strong suitability of deep learning for kidney stone classification though several important limitations warrant consideration. The AUC value of 0.987 across all models while demonstrating robust performance suggests potential ceiling effects in dataset that merit further investigation. Although rigorous train-validation-test splits were maintained throughout the study, the absence of confidence intervals limits the statistical interpretation of the results. The perfectly balanced 50:50 class distribution while ideal for model training and comparison does not reflect real world clinical prevalence rates and may artificially inflate performance metrics. Additionally, the reduction of image resolution to 64×64 pixels, though computationally efficient, may have removed subtle diagnostic features that could be clinically relevant in more challenging cases or for detecting smaller stones. Despite these limitations, the clinical relevance remains substantial. The near perfect sensitivity (99.47-99.67\%) is particularly valuable for minimizing missed diagnoses in clinical practice, while the high specificity (97.75-99.44\%) helps reduce unnecessary follow up procedures and patient anxiety. The computational efficiency of MLP (0.58 seconds per epoch) further enhances its potential for real-time clinical applications.


