\subsection{Limitations and Statistical Considerations}
While results demonstrate exceptional performance but several limitations warrant consideration as the consistent AUC value of 0.987 across all models while impressive, suggests potential ceiling effects in dataset. The 50:50 class distribution while ideal for model development, may not reflect real-world prevalence rates. Additionally, the image resolution reduction to 64×64 pixels, though computationally efficient, may have removed subtle diagnostic features that could be relevant in more challenging cases.

The absence of confidence intervals and external validation represents a limitation in establishing generalizability. Future work should incorporate bootstrap confidence intervals and multi-center validation to strengthen the statistical robustness of these findings. Furthermore, the binary classification framework limits clinical applicability, as real-world diagnostic scenarios often require differentiation between multiple stone types and normal anatomical variations.




\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
This study demonstrates the effectiveness of deep learning models for kidney stone classification, with MLP achieving the highest accuracy of 99.67\% while providing optimal computational efficiency. All models achieved accuracy over 98.95\%, highlighting their potential for clinical deployment. The contrastive evaluation provides valuable insights into performance complexity trade offs for medical imaging applications. Future work will integrate Explainable AI \textbf{SHAP, LIME} for model interpretability and develop diagnostic tools for practical implementation and Multi-center validation and stone type classification will further enhance clinical applicability.

